  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does the coil spring do?",
      "a": "The coil spring holds up the car's weight and squashes when a wheel hits a bump. It stores the bump's energy and pushes the wheel back down, so the body moves much less than the wheel.",
      "asks": [
        "What are car springs for?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does a shock absorber do?",
      "a": "A shock absorber, or damper, stops the spring bouncing on and on. A piston forces oil through small holes, which turns the energy of the bounce into heat.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a damper?",
        "Why does a car need shock absorbers?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does suspension help a car grip the road?",
      "a": "A tyre can only grip while it touches the road. Suspension keeps each wheel pressed down over bumps, so the car can still steer and brake on rough surfaces.",
      "asks": [
        "Is suspension only for comfort?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens when shock absorbers wear out?",
      "a": "With worn dampers the car keeps bouncing after a bump, leans more in corners and dips its nose when braking. The tyres can lose contact with the road, so stopping distances get longer.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you know if shocks are worn?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the suspension arm?",
      "a": "The suspension arm is a hinged link joining the wheel to the car body. It lets the wheel swing up and down while holding it in place side to side.",
      "asks": [
        "What holds the wheel to the car?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The tyres themselves act as small air springs, soaking up the smallest bumps.",
    "The parts below the springs, such as wheels and brakes, are called unsprung mass; making them lighter gives a smoother ride.",
    "A shock absorber turns the energy of bouncing into heat in its oil."
  ],
